

Documentation for Hypersignal DSP software is as follows:

1) Users Manual.

2) Tutorial.  Contains several practical examples of "real-world" DSP algorithm
   development, function generation, filter design, instrumentation
   measurements, waveform display and editing, etc.

3) On-line HELP (activated by the 'F1', '?', or 'H' keys at anytime during
   Hypersignal operation).

4) Addendum to Users Manual (provided as disk file ADDENDUM.DOC).

   PLEASE AT LEAST SKIM THE ADDENDUM.DOC FILE BEFORE USING HYPERSIGNAL!!!

5) Disk file HS_REV2.DOC, which contains a chronological listing of all
   minor-version bug fixes and enhancements for history of Hypersignal.


INSTALLING AND RUNNING HYPERSIGNAL:

1) The basic installation procedure is as follows:

   - Create a subdirectory called HSIGNAL (or similar), and copy *.* from
     distribution diskettes #1 and #2 into this subdirectory

   - Create a subdirectory called WAVEFORM (or similar) and copy *.* from
     distribution diskette #3 into this subdirectory

2) Generally, the easiest way to enable mouse operation with Hypersignal
   software is to run your MOUSE.COM program (comes with purchase of mouse)
   PRIOR TO RUNNING HYPERSIGNAL, then select the "memory-resident driver"
   selection in the Hypersignal Mouse Configuration menu.  Some mice are
   supported directly through the COM1 and COM2 serial ports and can be
   selected as such in the Mouse Configuration menu.

   If you don't have a mouse, Hypersignal will probably display a message
   indicating that no mouse hardware was found in your system, and set the
   program for keyboard-only.  If not, then be sure to select the
   "keyboard-only" option in the "Mouse Configuration" menu selection.  Doing
   so greatly "speeds" up the keyboard response for zoom and other
   waveform-cursor operations.

3) Hypersignal automatically detects and uses an 80x87 math coprocessor if
   present in your system.  You can override automatic 80x87 usage through
   a DOS environment variable, if necessary.  See the Users Manual.

4) Use the HSW.BAT and HSP.BAT files to run Hypersignal-Workstation and
   Hypersignal-Plus.  They will keep you out of trouble as far as making
   sure the grahics library is installed in system memory before Hypersignal
   runs and removing it from system memory when Hypersignal is done.

   For example, to run Hypersignal-Workstation, type:

   HSW <cr>

5) Use the 'F2' key inside any Hypersignal waveform display to activate the
   on-line print function.  Doing so brings up a pop-up menu in which you
   can specify a screen dump with scale factors, or an "EMS memory
   regeneration" with image dimensions controlled in inches, which can produce
   a publication-quality print-out depending on the resolution of your
   printer.  Other parameters such as background, rotation, and number of
   copies can also be specified.  Nearly 200 different printers are supported.

